# 2. Basic Data Types in python.

#   |   Data Type   |   Exmaple     |   Meaning         |
#   |   str         |   "Vineet"    |   Text            |
#   |   int         |   19          |   Whole number    |
#   |   float       |   85.5        |   Decimal number  |
#   |   bool        |   True        |   True or False   |

# Function:- to check the data type of variable in python is -- type()

name = "Vineet"
age = 19
marks = 85.5
is_student = True

print(type(name))
print(type(age))
print(type(marks))
print(type(is_student))

# Output:-
# <class 'str'>
# <class 'int'>
# <class 'float'>
# <class ''bool'>

# 3. input() -- the function the use to take the input from user.

name = input("Enter the name: ")
print("Welcome", name)

# when code run it will give you:- Enter your name: 
# Enter your name : Vineet

# Output:-
# Welcome Vineet

# 4. Important concept of -- input() and Numbers

# In Python, the value returned by input() is always a string ( str ) by default, even if the user enters a number.

# Incorrect Approach
a = input("Enter first number: ")
b = input("Enter second number: ")

print(a + b)

# If you enter 10 and 20, the result will be 1020 because Python is joining the two values as text instead of adding them as numbers.

# Correct Approach
a = int(input("Enter first number: "))
b = int(input("Enter second nnumber: "))

print("Sum =", a + b)

# Output:-
# Enter first number: 10
# Enter second number: 20
# Sum = 30

# int() converts a value into an integer ( a whole number ).
# float() converts a value into a floating-point number (a decimal number ).

# For example, use float(input()) when you want to accept decimal numbers such as 10.5.


